WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.420
Good morning John, as you can see I still have this annoying

00:00:02.760 --> 00:00:04.680
clingy face fur attached to my body

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:07.720
and people are starting to tell me that I look like Westley from Princess Bride,

00:00:07.720 --> 00:00:09.696
and I'm starting to feel like that's not so bad.

00:00:09.696 --> 00:00:12.840
Feel like I look like a cat though! Cats have fuzzy faces!

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:14.160
And it pokes me, constantly poking me.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:17.060
I don't even know how I handle the hair on my head anymore.

00:00:17.060 --> 00:00:20.220
But, due to an overwhelming influx of comments

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:22.400
about how much people like my goatee

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:24.300
and how much they don't want me to shave it,

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:26.760
I've kept it for now.

00:00:26.760 --> 00:00:29.420
And if you're wondering how I got this excellent sort of shape to it,

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.980
it's because hair does not grow here. It's just me being a little boy.

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:36.660
John, this is gonna sound like a weird topic change, but have you ever heard of physiognomy?

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.340
I learned about physiognomy while searching, uh, the website of Kim McGuire,

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:43.280
who played Hatchet-Face in Cry-Baby, who I'm kind of obsessed with.

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:47.500
Also in that Wikipedia page, I found out that John Waters cast the part of Hatchet-Face with a one-line ad:

00:00:47.500 --> 00:00:52.380
"Wanted: Girl with a good body and an alarming face who is proud of it."

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.760
Anyway! Physiognomy is the assessment of a person's character by looking at their face or body.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:04.280
This is, on its face, [puppet interrupts:] That was intentional, wasn't it? [Hank:] Yes. Yeah, it was intentional, but I wasn't going to call attention to it.

00:01:04.280 --> 00:01:08.620
Anyway, that is, on its face, wrong. We shouldn't judge people by what they look like, we all know that!

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:11.960
Except, in some cases, you kind of can.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:15.360
For example, aggressive men tend to have wider faces

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:18.040
because they had more testosterone in their system when they were growing.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:23.960
Gay men are three times more likely to have counterclockwise hair whirls, and that's just weird!

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:28.100
I checked—I had Katherine check mine—I'm clockwise. So now we know for sure.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.200
When a composite of thousands of religious women were put together,

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:35.240
73% of people identified that image as a composite of a religious person.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.320
But these are all this little tips, they're not something that we actually take into account, right?

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:41.640
Well, whether or not we *should* judge a book by its cover, we do.

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:47.300
Men with faces that more closely resemble children are less likely to be convicted in a court of law

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:49.100
than men who look more masculine.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.240
And yet!—babyfaced men are more likely to be convicted of negligence.

00:01:53.320 --> 00:01:56.460
Psychologists have studied this, and they've determined that within a tenth of a second

00:01:56.460 --> 00:02:00.000
of seeing a person for the first time, we have already made a series of judgments,

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.340
not just about how attractive they are,

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.000
but how trustworthy they are, how assertive they are, how funny they're going to be.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.460
We're built to make these snap judgments about each other because at some point in our history,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:11.080
it was necessary for our survival to do so.

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:14.280
And now we build even more signals into the way we style our hair,

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:17.980
the shoes that we wear, the socks, the clothes, tattoos, and piercings;

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:22.680
all a way to give cultural cues about what kind of person we are.

00:02:22.680 --> 00:02:26.220
All of this brings me back to the goatee.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.480
Does this thing change who I am? Does it change the way people see me?

00:02:29.480 --> 00:02:32.260
And if people see me differently, do I see myself differently?

00:02:32.260 --> 00:02:34.440
Do I act differently because that's the way that they see me?

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.500
Not so far, I think... Do I seem different? I don't know!

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.980
If I had stuck with contacts in high school and looked like this all the time,

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:44.720
would I have been a different person? Is this guy not me?

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:48.820
What it comes down to is this is too big of a decision for me to make alone.

00:02:48.820 --> 00:02:53.420
And it's also too good of an opportunity to raise money for the Foundation to Decrease Worldsuck!

00:02:53.430 --> 00:02:57.390
We need to increase the coffers now, in preparation for the Project for Awesome.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:02.740
So if you have an opinion on whether or not I should keep this face fuzz intact or not,

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:08.960
go to fightworldsuck.org right now. And you can vote, but not with your clicks. With your dollars!

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:12.360
And I will tell you that PayPal takes like 33 cents, no matter what.

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:15.600
So if you donate a dollar, 33 cents of that's going to PayPal.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.060
If you donate five, 33 cents of that's going to PayPal.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.560
So, percentage-wise, you should probably avoid just doing a dollar.

00:03:22.560 --> 00:03:27.300
And if you do less than 33 cents, they take all of it! So that's great.

00:03:27.620 --> 00:03:30.580
So put your money in the "shave it" pile or put your money in the "keep it" pile,

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:34.140
and based on that, in my next video I will let you know whether or not

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:36.420
I'm going to do the "Neanderthal November" thing.

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:37.860
John, one final note:

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:42.300
Congratulations on signing a hundred and fifty thousand copies of The Fault in Our Stars.

00:03:42.300 --> 00:03:47.380
You're done! Go on vacation, except don't, 'cause we need you to make videos to make us laugh.

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.060
I'll see you on Monday.

